WEBVTT

00:00.220 --> 00:01.760
- [Instructor] We're told that Lainey runs

00:01.760 --> 00:04.160
a string of lights from
the ground straight up

00:04.160 --> 00:08.980
to a door frame that is 2.5 meters tall.

00:08.980 --> 00:11.200
Then they run the rest of the string

00:11.200 --> 00:13.870
in a straight line to
a point on the ground

00:13.870 --> 00:17.530
that is six meters from
the base of the door frame.

00:17.530 --> 00:22.410
There are 10 lights per
meter of the string.

00:22.410 --> 00:25.940
How many total lights are on the string?

00:25.940 --> 00:26.810
So I'll pause this video

00:26.810 --> 00:28.460
and see if you can work this out.

00:29.720 --> 00:31.330
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:31.330 --> 00:34.080
I think this one warrants
some type of a diagram.

00:34.080 --> 00:38.740
So let me draw this door
frame that looks like this.

00:38.740 --> 00:42.700
And this door frame is 2.5 meters tall.

00:42.700 --> 00:44.600
So that's its height right over there.

00:44.600 --> 00:46.110
And what they're going to do,

00:46.110 --> 00:49.950
what Lainey's doing is she
is string up these lights,

00:49.950 --> 00:51.850
so that's this yellow right over here.

00:51.850 --> 00:53.950
So it goes up to the
top of that door frame

00:53.950 --> 00:56.960
and then they run the rest of the light

00:56.960 --> 00:58.960
in a straight line to
a point on the ground

00:58.960 --> 01:02.070
that is six meters from
the base of the door frame.

01:02.070 --> 01:03.930
So let me show a point that is six meters

01:03.930 --> 01:05.340
from the base of the door frame.

01:05.340 --> 01:09.930
So it looks something
like, maybe like that.

01:09.930 --> 01:12.740
So this distance right
over here is six meters.

01:12.740 --> 01:14.390
So they run the rest of the light

01:15.840 --> 01:17.440
from the top of the door frame

01:17.440 --> 01:20.570
to that point to that six meters away.

01:20.570 --> 01:21.900
So the yellow right over here,

01:21.900 --> 01:23.440
that is the light.

01:23.440 --> 01:25.130
And so we had to figure out

01:25.130 --> 01:26.840
how many total lights are on the string.

01:26.840 --> 01:29.060
So the way I would tackle
it is first of all,

01:29.060 --> 01:32.750
I wanna figure out how
long is the total string.

01:32.750 --> 01:33.980
And to figure that out,

01:33.980 --> 01:34.813
I just need to figure out,

01:34.813 --> 01:37.610
okay, it's gonna be 2.5 plus
whatever the hypothenuse

01:37.610 --> 01:39.380
is of this right triangle.

01:39.380 --> 01:41.980
I think it's safe to assume
that this is a standard house

01:41.980 --> 01:44.550
where door frames are at a
right angle to the floor.

01:44.550 --> 01:45.490
And so we have to figure out

01:45.490 --> 01:46.730
the length of this hypothenuse

01:46.730 --> 01:49.890
and if we know that, plus this 2.5 meters,

01:49.890 --> 01:53.200
then we know how long the
entire string of lights are.

01:53.200 --> 01:55.240
And then we just have to
really multiply it by 10

01:55.240 --> 01:58.370
because there's 10 lights
per meter of the string.

01:58.370 --> 01:59.750
So let's do that.

01:59.750 --> 02:01.510
So how do we figure out
the hypothenuse here?

02:01.510 --> 02:03.350
Well, of course, we would
use Pythagorean theorem.

02:03.350 --> 02:04.540
So let's call this,

02:04.540 --> 02:07.810
and also call this h for hypothenuse.

02:07.810 --> 02:09.830
We know that the hypothenuse squared

02:09.830 --> 02:14.830
is equal to 2.5 squared plus six squared.

02:17.680 --> 02:22.100
So this is going to be
equal to 6.25 plus 36,

02:26.280 --> 02:31.280
which is equal to 42.25 or we could say

02:31.440 --> 02:33.180
that the hypothenuse is going to be equal

02:33.180 --> 02:37.850
to the square root of 42.25.

02:37.850 --> 02:39.750
And I could get my
calculator out at this point,

02:39.750 --> 02:42.360
but I'll actually just
keep using this expression

02:42.360 --> 02:45.090
to figure out the total number of lights.

02:45.090 --> 02:47.040
So what's the total length of the string?

02:47.040 --> 02:48.200
We have to be careful here.

02:48.200 --> 02:49.033
A lot of folks would say,

02:49.033 --> 02:50.360
"Oh, I figure out the hypothenuse,

02:50.360 --> 02:52.350
let me just multiply it that by 10."

02:52.350 --> 02:54.820
In fact, my brain almost
did that just now.

02:54.820 --> 02:57.480
But we gotta realize
that the entire string

02:57.480 --> 02:59.313
is the hypothenuse plus this 2.5.

03:00.380 --> 03:02.250
So the whole string length,

03:02.250 --> 03:03.190
Write this way.

03:03.190 --> 03:08.190
String length is equal to 2.5

03:10.960 --> 03:14.453
plus the square root of 42.25.

03:18.050 --> 03:20.800
And then we would just
multiply it that time 10

03:20.800 --> 03:23.400
to get the total number of lights.

03:23.400 --> 03:26.650
So now let's actually
get the calculator out.

03:26.650 --> 03:31.610
So we have 42.25 and then we would've take

03:31.610 --> 03:34.430
the square root of that.

03:34.430 --> 03:37.483
Gets us to 6.5 and then
we add the other 2.5,

03:38.410 --> 03:41.860
plus 2.5 equals that.

03:41.860 --> 03:44.570
That's the total length of string.

03:44.570 --> 03:47.240
So the total length of
string is nine meters

03:47.240 --> 03:49.530
and there're 10 lights per meter.

03:49.530 --> 03:51.600
So the number of lights,

03:51.600 --> 03:56.600
number of lights is equal to
nine total meters of string

04:00.010 --> 04:05.010
times 10 lights per meter,

04:05.220 --> 04:09.420
which would give us 90 lights.

04:09.420 --> 04:10.960
Now some of you might debate,

04:10.960 --> 04:12.360
if you think really deeply about it,

04:12.360 --> 04:14.620
is if you have a light
right at the beginning,

04:14.620 --> 04:17.200
if these were kind of
set up like a fence post,

04:17.200 --> 04:20.680
then maybe you can argue
that there's one extra light

04:20.680 --> 04:22.990
in there, but for the sake of this,

04:22.990 --> 04:24.150
I kind of view this as,

04:24.150 --> 04:26.430
on average there're 10 lights per meter

04:26.430 --> 04:29.800
and so if it's a nine meter string,

04:29.800 --> 04:32.743
nine times 10 is 90 lights.
